# Project: Lakeside patient scheduling voice agent

This is a live interview build. I have ~2.5 hours and I will be asked to
explain every line of this code to a panel of engineers afterwards.

## Pipecat version and providers

This project uses pipecat-ai 0.0.108, NOT 1.x. Only use APIs that
exist in the installed package. Read site-packages to confirm class
names and constructor arguments before using them. Never suggest
pipecat 1.x imports or patterns.

LLM is Anthropic. STT and TTS are Deepgram. No Cartesia, no OpenAI.

## The most important rule

Write code I can defend out loud. If there is a clever way and an obvious
way, write the obvious way. I would rather read 15 lines I understand
instantly than 5 lines I have to reason about.

Never generate a function I have not asked for. Never add a feature
"while we're here". If you think something is missing, say so in a
comment or in chat — do not build it.

## Code style

- Explicit over idiomatic. Full `if` / `else` blocks, not ternaries or
  one-line comprehensions with conditionals.
- Descriptive variable names. `matching_providers`, not `mp` or `result`.
- No list/dict comprehensions longer than one simple clause. Use a loop.
- No `lambda`. Define a named function.
- No walrus operator, no `functools.reduce`, no metaclasses, no decorators
  beyond the ones a framework requires.
- Early returns for guard clauses are fine and preferred over nesting.
- Type hints on function signatures. Not on every local variable.
- Docstring on every public function: one line saying what it does, and a
  line saying what it returns when that is not obvious.
- Comments explain WHY, never WHAT. If the what is unclear, rename things.

## Architectural invariants — do not violate these

1. **The LLM never calls a write tool.** On the confirmation stage the
   model's only job is to return structured output indicating whether the
   patient confirmed. Python code reads that field and performs the write.
   `book_appointment`, `reschedule_appointment` and `cancel_appointment`
   are called from `agent/state_machine.py`, never exposed in a tool
   schema handed to the model.

2. **Tools are gated by stage.** The tool schemas passed to the model are
   built per stage from the state machine. The model never sees the full
   tool list.

3. **The agent core is transport-agnostic.** `agent/core.py` takes text
   and returns text. It must never import anything from `voice/`. Voice is
   a front end. I need to be able to run the whole agent from `cli.py`
   with no audio.

4. **Every write is idempotent.** Writes take an idempotency key generated
   by code (not the model) from session id + the thing being acted on.

5. **All clinic data access goes through `adapter/`.** No module outside
   `adapter/` reads a CSV or knows a column name. The adapter is the only
   place that knows the data is files rather than an EHR.

6. **Normalise on the way in.** Specialty labels in the source data are
   inconsistent ("cardio", "Heart", "Orthopaedics", "OBGYN"). The adapter
   maps them to the canonical list from clinic_rules.json. Nothing
   downstream ever sees a raw label.

7. **Safety is separate code, not a prompt instruction.** Red-flag
   detection runs on the patient transcript before the agent turn, in
   `agent/safety.py`, independent of the model. The grounding check runs
   on agent output before it is spoken.

8. **The agent states no fact that did not come from a tool result in
   this conversation.** Provider names, dates, times, locations.

## Project layout

Put code where it belongs. Do not create new top-level directories.

    data/                  the clinic's CSV and JSON files, read only
    adapter/
      scheduling_adapter.py   loads data, the only module that reads files
      normalize.py            specialty/location label normalisation
    agent/
      core.py                 text in, text out. the agent loop
      state_machine.py        stages, allowed tools, exit conditions, writes
      tools.py                tool schemas and implementations
      safety.py               red flag detection, grounding check
    voice/
      pipeline.py             pipecat wiring. imports agent/, never reverse
    tests/
      scenarios.py            scripted conversations, assert on outcomes
    cli.py                    text REPL against agent core
    README.md

## Tool design rules

- Search tools take every filter as optional. `search_providers` must
  accept name, specialty, location, language, gender, insurance and
  accepting_new in any combination. Never force a caller to go through
  specialty to find a doctor by name.
- IDs cross tool boundaries, never names. Return provider_id, take
  provider_id.
- Return small, ranked results. Never return more than 5 slots.
- Errors are recovery instructions, not failures. Return
  "Dr Kim is not accepting new patients; Dr Lee in the same department is"
  rather than an empty list or an exception.

## Testing

Scripted scenarios in `tests/scenarios.py` run against the agent core in
text mode. Assert on outcomes — did it book the right slot, did it
escalate, did it refuse the write — never on exact wording.

## What not to do

- Do not add a web framework, a database, an ORM, or a message queue.
- Do not add retry/caching/observability libraries. A print statement and
  a dict are fine for today.
- Do not write async code unless the framework requires it at that point.
- Do not catch exceptions broadly to make something pass. Let it fail
  loudly so I can see it.
- Do not write a README section about features that do not exist.
